    Del Norte Project Approved: What West Covina Residents Need to Know

232 Homes Approved, Park Land Added, and Questions That Still Need Answers

WEST COVINA, CA — After months of public meetings, revisions, neighborhood concerns, and debate over parks, traffic, housing density, and Little League fields, the West Covina City Council voted to approve the Del Norte residential development project on June 16, 2026.

The approval allows construction of 232 homes on the former Del Norte School property at 1501 W. Del Norte Street, while also requiring park land dedication, park fees, and first-time homebuyer assistance contributions.

Supporters described the project as an opportunity to expand homeownership and help the City meet state housing obligations.

Opponents argued the project increases density, traffic, parking pressure, and long-term maintenance obligations while potentially reducing recreational capacity at Del Norte Park.

The vote may be complete, but the conversation surrounding Del Norte is far from over.


---

Executive Summary

Approved Project Includes:

✅ 232 residential units

✅ 34 detached single-family homes

✅ 198 attached townhomes

✅ 1.45 acres of park land dedicated to the City

✅ $961,350 park fee payment

✅ Up to $250,000 in field improvements

✅ First-time homebuyer assistance funding

✅ New roadway connections and site infrastructure

Major Questions Remain:

❓ Will the baseball field function as a true replacement field?

❓ Where will the $961,350 park fee ultimately be spent?

❓ Has Del Norte been studied together with the City Yard proposal?

❓ What long-term maintenance costs will the City inherit?

❓ Will the homes be affordable to most West Covina families?


---

What Is the Del Norte Project?

The Del Norte project is a residential development proposed by Meritage Homes on the former Del Norte School property.

The site consists of approximately 13.6 acres located near Del Norte Street and Sunset Avenue adjacent to Del Norte Park.

The approved project includes:

232 residential units

34 detached homes

198 attached townhomes

Private internal streets

Community amenities

New roadway connections

Park dedication to the City


The project residents saw approved was significantly different from what was originally proposed.


---

How the Project Changed

One of the most important parts of the story is that Del Norte went through multiple revisions before reaching the Council.

Original Proposal

282 units

Included four-story buildings


Revised Proposal

Reduced to 260 units


Final Approved Version

Reduced further to 232 units

Four-story buildings removed

Increased park dedication

Additional neighborhood compatibility measures


According to the developer, many of these changes were made in response to concerns raised by residents, Little League supporters, City staff, and elected officials.


---

Why the Project Was Proposed

A recurring theme throughout the discussion was California's housing requirements.

West Covina's current Regional Housing Needs Allocation (RHNA) requires the City to plan for:

5,346 housing units

According to figures discussed during the meeting:

Approximately 511 units have been completed

Roughly 4,835 units remain


Supporters argued that the City must continue adding housing opportunities or risk potential consequences from the State.

Opponents argued that compliance should not come at the expense of community amenities and neighborhood character.


---

The Housing Debate

One of the most discussed questions during the hearing was affordability.

Residents repeatedly asked:

> Who can actually afford these homes?



During the meeting, Councilmember Tony Wu asked the developer about anticipated pricing.

Meritage Homes responded that based on current market conditions:

Estimated Townhome Prices

Mid-$600,000 range


Estimated Detached Home Prices

Mid-$800,000 range


Actual future prices remain unknown and will depend on market conditions when homes are sold.

The discussion highlighted a broader debate:

Supporters view the project as an opportunity for homeownership.

Critics question whether those prices are attainable for many West Covina residents.


---

First-Time Homebuyer Assistance

One of the changes negotiated during the approval process involved increasing assistance for future homebuyers.

The final approval requires:

Homebuyer Assistance Contribution

$25,000 per qualifying unit

Up to 10% of project units


This contribution was increased from the originally proposed $15,000 amount.

The goal is to help moderate-income households and first-time homebuyers access ownership opportunities.


---

The Baseball Field Debate

The baseball field became the most emotional issue discussed throughout the process.

What Was Approved

The developer will dedicate:

1.45 acres

To become part of Del Norte Park


The field will ultimately be transferred to the City after required improvements are completed.


---

Why Residents Objected

Little League supporters argued:

A larger field would better serve older age divisions

The approved field may primarily accommodate younger players

Recreational capacity could be reduced


Many residents viewed the field as a community asset that should be preserved as closely as possible to its previous form.


---

Developer Response

Meritage argued that expanding the field further would significantly affect:

Fire access

Site circulation

Parking

Project design

Housing layout


The developer maintained that the site is constrained by available land and school district property boundaries.


---

The Unresolved Question

The central issue remains:

> Is the City receiving a true replacement field or a smaller facility serving fewer age groups?



Residents and Little League supporters remain divided on the answer.


---

Park Dedication and Park Fees

The project provides two separate park-related benefits.

Park Land Dedication

Meritage will dedicate:

1.45 acres

To the City of West Covina


The land will become part of Del Norte Park.


---

Park-In-Lieu Fee

The developer must also pay:

$961,350

This payment is commonly known as a Quimby Fee.

These fees exist because new development increases demand on parks and recreational facilities.


---

Why the Fee Was Reduced

According to City calculations:

The original fee formula generated a fee approaching:

$2.5 million to $3 million


However, the dedicated park land generated approximately:

$1.58 million in credit


Reducing the final fee obligation to:

$961,350


---

What Residents Want to Know

The most common question raised during the discussion was simple:

> Where will the money go?



Councilmembers discussed possibilities including:

Dog park improvements

Little League facilities

Snack bar upgrades

Additional park improvements


At this time, no formal allocation has been approved.


---

Additional Field Improvements

Separate from the park fee, the City required the developer to spend up to:

$250,000

For field preparation prior to dedication.

Improvements include:

Grading

Irrigation

Sod installation


The intent is to ensure the field is usable before being transferred to the City.


---

Traffic Concerns

Traffic remained one of the most frequently discussed concerns.

The applicant stated:

Traffic studies were completed

City traffic engineers participated

The studies analyzed the larger 282-unit proposal

The approved 232-unit project is smaller than what was studied


The developer also stated that:

A left-turn pocket from Sunset Avenue was added

Fair-share transportation contributions will be required

Multiple intersections were evaluated


Despite those assurances, some residents remain concerned about neighborhood traffic impacts once the project is occupied.


---

The Question That Was Never Fully Answered

One of the most significant moments during the discussion involved cumulative impacts.

Residents repeatedly asked whether the City had studied Del Norte together with the proposed City Yard redevelopment.

Del Norte

232 units

City Yard

141 units

Combined

373 units

Residents asked whether the City had evaluated:

Combined traffic impacts

Combined public safety impacts

Combined park impacts

Combined infrastructure demands


No direct answer to that specific question was provided during the discussion.

As a result, it remains one of the largest unanswered questions moving forward.


---

Parking Concerns

Parking generated significant discussion as well.

The project provides:

526 parking spaces

Two-car garages for all units

Guest parking

Additional street parking opportunities


To address concerns, the Council added requirements that:

Garages be used for vehicle storage

Guest parking permits be implemented

HOA rules reinforce parking requirements


Some residents remain skeptical that these measures will fully prevent future overflow parking.


---

Final Vote

The project passed on a 3-2 vote.

In Support

Mayor Letty Lopez-Viado

Councilmember Rosario Diaz

Councilmember Tony Wu


In Opposition

Mayor Pro Tem Ollie Cantos

Councilmember Brian Gutierrez


Supporters emphasized:

Housing needs

Homeownership opportunities

State housing obligations

Project improvements made during review


Opponents focused on:

Recreational impacts

Long-term consequences

Traffic concerns

Unanswered questions



---

What Happens Next?

Approval does not mean construction begins immediately.

The project must still move through:

Final engineering

Final tract map approvals

Permit processing

Site preparation

Infrastructure installation


Future discussions regarding park improvements, fee allocation, and implementation details are expected to continue.


---

Questions Residents Should Continue Asking

1. Where will the $961,350 park fee ultimately be spent?


2. How much will annual maintenance of the dedicated park land cost?


3. Will the baseball field support the same age groups as before?


4. Is the $250,000 field improvement requirement separate from the park fee?


5. Has the City evaluated the combined impact of Del Norte and the City Yard proposal?


6. How will traffic change once the project is occupied?


7. Will the homes be affordable to typical West Covina families?




---

Bottom Line

The Del Norte project represents one of the largest housing approvals in West Covina in recent years.

The City gained:

New housing opportunities

Additional park land

Nearly $1 million in park fees

Homebuyer assistance funding

New infrastructure improvements


At the same time, residents continue raising questions about:

Traffic

Parking

Recreational capacity

Long-term maintenance costs

Combined development impacts


The vote may be complete.

The questions are not.

And those answers will likely shape future discussions about development, parks, public spending, and growth throughout West Covina.

